{
  "_comment": "TrustOps topology (design A 4.1, 4.5), imported at boot via rabbitmq.conf. RETRY (TTL + DLX requeue): on a handler exception with x-attempt < 3 the consumer republishes the message to exchange 'retry' with routing key '<queue>' and x-attempt + 1, then acks the original. '<queue>.retry' has no consumer; it holds the message for x-message-ttl (30 s) and on expiry dead-letters it through the default exchange ('') with routing key '<queue>', i.e. straight back onto the work queue: a delayed redelivery without the delayed-message plugin. PARK: when x-attempt reaches 3, or the envelope fails validation (poison), the consumer publishes to exchange 'dlx' with routing key '<queue>' and acks; '<queue>.dlq' keeps it with no TTL until 'make dlq-replay q=<queue>'. Work queues also carry x-dead-letter-exchange 'dlx', so a reject(requeue=false), or the quorum delivery-limit (5, policy fcrm-quorum-safety) after repeated consumer crashes, lands in the same DLQ. Policy fcrm-quorum-safety makes dead-lettering at-least-once (requires overflow reject-publish), so nothing is dropped between queues. Clients must declare these queues passively (or with exactly these arguments): ops owns the topology. No users here: ops/rabbitmq/entrypoint.sh adds the single RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER from .env (hashed) to a runtime copy at start.",
  "rabbit_version": "4.0.9",
  "rabbitmq_version": "4.0.9",
  "vhosts": [
    {
      "name": "/"
    }
  ],
  "policies": [
    {
      "vhost": "/",
      "name": "fcrm-quorum-safety",
      "pattern": "^(caseflow|case-service|policy-service|evals|agent-worker|ingestion-worker|context-worker)\\.",
      "apply-to": "queues",
      "priority": 0,
      "definition": {
        "dead-letter-strategy": "at-least-once",
        "overflow": "reject-publish",
        "delivery-limit": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "vhost": "/",
      "name": "fcrm-dlq-keep",
      "pattern": "\\.dlq$",
      "apply-to": "queues",
      "priority": 10,
      "definition": {
        "delivery-limit": -1
      }
    }
  ],
